at you can use khan academy  on uh for on the order of a few tens of  billions which sounds like a large  number but that's about  point that's about one percent of the  amount of money that's been spent in the  stimulus packages just over the past few  few months and to loot back to the main  topic i mean where is broadband access a  problem it's much more problem among  lower income people than upper income  people  yeah one one question that this is  coming uh from um you know it's going  back to one of your other levels on the  education i i do want to ask this  because it's a thought experiment that  i've had with friends you know just  thinking about the issue jenny scoble  from facebook asks but let's say  everyone gets a college education will  there really be jobs for all of those  people i mean you could imagine what if  everyone had a phd tomorrow  will there really be that many jobs for  for those for those folks  so i love this question i get the  version this question a lot when i'm out  speaking i would actually ask people to  flip it and go not forwards in time but  backwards in time why is it necessary  that everyone gets a high school  education why is it necessary that  everyone gets an 8th grade education why  did we as a society decide that 13 years  of public education was essentially the  minimum that all people should have and  that's not just a hypothetical question  there was a real live debate in this  country in the early 20th century and  around the world about this and there  were people who said come on let's be  realistic  most americans don't need to go to  um high school they need to learn to  read and that's about it we the high  school should be for for the the top  echelons of society it's a waste of time  and money to send ordinary workers to  high school and then there were people  the high school movement which  was a national movement a sort of  national local movement maybe strongest  at first in iowa who said no no no  education will pay off if we create more  educated workers more educated citizens  our society will be healthier and there  will end up being jobs for for many of  those people and that second group  really won the bet in a route  europe for a long time kept education um  high school education is more of an  elite thing we went to mass high school  education and it built so many um great  american industries um it built  agriculture to have educated farmers it  built hollywood  it built american manufacturing it built  one industry after another and so if 13  years was the amount of mass education  that made sense in the early and mid  20th century  why is it that we think that number  wouldn't have gone up almost 100 years  later and i think clearly when you think  about how much more advanced technology  is that number  has gone up and so thinking about 15 or  17 years as the right number for mass  education makes sense and my instinct is  is if we got there much has happened 100  years ago we would find all kinds of  jobs we didn't even know existed for our  more educated citizenry and i don't  think there's a natural limit that says  well nine wasn't enough education 13  years was was enough education but  anything more than 13 would end up being  wasted  yup no and obviously you're preaching to  the choir with when you're talking to me  about the way i think about it is  there is some logic to the argument that  jenny is making that you know a degree  doesn't automatically convert into a job  but from a societal scale the and it's  you know we use years as a proxy and we  use high school and college as a proxy  but it's really can we get people to  that level of critical thinking can we  get them to that level of skill so they  can leverage the technology around them  leverage you know understand the  complexity of the environments so they  can either plug into jobs that already  exist or will exist at 10 years or even  more interesting so they can create new  jobs